WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.466 --> 00:00:02.967
Hi there! This is Steve Kaufmann.

00:00:02.970 --> 00:00:09.200
Today I want to talk about something that I often
 think about, when it comes to language learning.

00:00:09.839 --> 00:00:15.264
And that is the relationship between
language learning and the jigsaw puzzle.

00:00:16.071 --> 00:00:22.397
And I think of it all the time as I'm
learning Russian and, all of a sudden,

00:00:22.397 --> 00:00:26.515
a new piece of the puzzle,
basically, clicks for me.

00:00:26.944 --> 00:00:32.904
And the relationship is this: that with a jigsaw
puzzle you begin with a lot of little pieces

00:00:32.904 --> 00:00:37.335
that don't look like anything;
there's no image, you have no idea

00:00:37.335 --> 00:00:40.519
how you can possibly get from
that to the completed picture.

00:00:40.986 --> 00:00:44.432
But as you proceed to put the
little pieces together, guessing;

00:00:44.432 --> 00:00:46.753
you find two that kind
of look like each other,

00:00:46.753 --> 00:00:49.376
that they belong together,
you put them together.

00:00:49.632 --> 00:00:54.160
And often by chance you find
one that fits in, and of course,

00:00:54.160 --> 00:01:02.059
the biggest thing about a jigsaw puzzle is:
the more of the picture that you start to see,

00:01:02.059 --> 00:01:07.651
the easier it is then to
find the pieces that fit in.

00:01:07.667 --> 00:01:10.318
You start to sense
what you're looking for.

00:01:10.849 --> 00:01:13.782
And in language learning
I've always felt that

00:01:13.782 --> 00:01:18.088
there are certain things that
I won't be able to make sense so,

00:01:18.096 --> 00:01:22.360
until I have a lot of the other
pieces of the puzzle in there.

00:01:22.376 --> 00:01:27.369
So, in the case of Russian,
some of the cases will...

00:01:27.369 --> 00:01:31.301
I will start to notice them.
And as they become obvious

00:01:31.301 --> 00:01:36.498
and I start to take them for granted, then
I focus on some other pieces of the puzzle.

00:01:36.498 --> 00:01:41.574
The same is true with, you know,
verbs in French or Spanish.

00:01:41.580 --> 00:01:45.782
If you're given a whole bunch
of verbs or a whole bunch of,

00:01:45.791 --> 00:01:49.034
in the case again, German
or Russian, noun endings,

00:01:49.043 --> 00:01:51.461
you can't possibly
absorb them.

00:01:51.479 --> 00:01:56.378
But as you put some of the pieces together,
then the other pieces start to fit in.

00:01:56.612 --> 00:02:03.179
And, in the case of verb endings, always
some tenses or some endings will remain more

00:02:03.179 --> 00:02:05.977
difficult than others, you may
always have trouble with them.

00:02:06.107 --> 00:02:13.005
But it's a lot easier to cope with that
when the other pieces are already in place.

00:02:13.005 --> 00:02:17.133
I mean the same is true with colors,
if you're presented with a whole list of colors

00:02:17.133 --> 00:02:20.664
you can't possibly absorb
all those names of colors.

00:02:20.718 --> 00:02:26.633
But as some of the colors start to slot in,
it then becomes easier to learn the other colors.

00:02:26.742 --> 00:02:30.034
The same with days of the week,
the same with months of the year,

00:02:30.034 --> 00:02:35.544
 the same with any aspect of the language
if it comes at you as one great whole set

00:02:35.544 --> 00:02:39.116
that you have to try to understand,
to learn as a set,

00:02:39.124 --> 00:02:42.512
the parts of the body or whatever.
It's very difficult to absorb it.

00:02:43.326 --> 00:02:46.476
Just like the loose pieces
of the jigsaw puzzle,

00:02:46.476 --> 00:02:52.343
but as some of the shape forms,
and the image start to take shape,

00:02:52.343 --> 00:02:55.772
then it becomes easier to
learn the other pieces.

00:02:55.856 --> 00:02:57.740
And I think it's important
to remember that

00:02:57.740 --> 00:03:00.935
to particularly when you think
you're making very little progress,

00:03:00.935 --> 00:03:05.521
slowly different things
start to slot into place.

00:03:05.521 --> 00:03:09.048
But in order for them to slot into place
you have to try to notice,

00:03:09.048 --> 00:03:13.572
you have to be consciously aware
of those pieces that you have

00:03:13.572 --> 00:03:16.892
 and those pieces that
you don't yet have.

00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:22.064
So, just a short comment
there on the importance of

00:03:22.559 --> 00:03:28.711
being aware that learning a language
is picking up a lot of bits and pieces

00:03:28.711 --> 00:03:33.575
very often incidentally and that the
more you have the more you can learn.

00:03:33.575 --> 00:03:38.648
And that's again another reason why
you need to keep at it, constantly,

00:03:38.648 --> 00:03:42.112
and focus largely on things
that you like doing and,

00:03:42.112 --> 00:03:46.759
 in particular, on consuming a lot
of content - listening and reading -

00:03:46.759 --> 00:03:49.317
or putting yourself in
situations where you,

00:03:49.317 --> 00:03:52.252
interacting with people
and you're struggling to find

00:03:52.252 --> 00:03:55.145
those missing pieces and
by struggling a few times,

00:03:55.145 --> 00:03:57.638
eventually, you'll start to
notice them somewhere

00:03:57.645 --> 00:04:00.845
and then they become part
of the language for you.

00:04:00.857 --> 00:04:02.700
I hope that's helpful
to some people.

